    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Worked Example: Turning Cases Into a Rate",
     "content": "Counting cases is not enough. A county with 512 cancers is not \"worse\" than a county with 300 if it has four times the people. You need a **rate**.\n\nTake an illustrative county. In 2023 its central registry counted **512** new invasive colorectal cancer cases among residents. Census estimates put the county population at **312,000**.\n\n**Crude incidence rate = (cases ÷ population) × 100,000**\n\n(512 ÷ 312,000) × 100,000 = **164.1 per 100,000**\n\nThat number is only interpretable because the numerator came from a **population-based** registry — every case in that county, not just the ones treated at one hospital — and the denominator came from the census. Numerator and denominator must describe the *same* population. Mismatch them and the rate is meaningless.\n\nOne more step follows: **age-adjustment** to the 2000 U.S. standard population, so an older county is not penalized for being old. You will do that arithmetic later in this course.\n\nThis one calculation is a doorway into everything registry data is used for:\n\n• **Incidence and survival trends** over time\n• **Treatment patterns** — who actually got guideline-concordant care\n• **Research** — cohort identification, clinical trial feasibility, outcomes studies\n• **Cancer control programs** — where to send screening vans and outreach dollars\n• **Quality benchmarking** — how your hospital compares to national peers",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">FROM CASE COUNT TO INCIDENCE RATE</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"23\">Illustrative county, invasive colorectal cancer, 2023</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"150\" width=\"460\" height=\"200\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">NUMERATOR</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"272\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"48\" font-weight=\"bold\">512</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"315\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"19\">new cases, from the registry</text><rect x=\"580\" y=\"150\" width=\"460\" height=\"200\" fill=\"rgba(20,184,166,0.15)\" stroke=\"#14b8a6\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"810\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14b8a6\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">DENOMINATOR</text><text x=\"810\" y=\"272\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"48\" font-weight=\"bold\">312,000</text><text x=\"810\" y=\"315\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"19\">residents, from the census</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"385\" width=\"980\" height=\"120\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"440\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-size=\"21\" font-weight=\"bold\">CRUDE INCIDENCE RATE</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"482\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"26\">(512 ÷ 312,000) × 100,000</text><rect x=\"250\" y=\"540\" width=\"600\" height=\"150\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"610\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"46\" font-weight=\"bold\">164.1</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"655\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">cases per 100,000 residents</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"745\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"21\">How it compares (illustrative)</text><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"1000\" x2=\"1000\" y2=\"1000\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"200\" y=\"836\" width=\"160\" height=\"164\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"280\" y=\"820\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">164.1</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"1035\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">County</text><rect x=\"470\" y=\"848\" width=\"160\" height=\"152\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"832\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">152.0</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"1035\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">State</text><rect x=\"740\" y=\"859\" width=\"160\" height=\"141\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"820\" y=\"843\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">141.0</text><text x=\"820\" y=\"1035\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Nation</text></svg>",
      "caption": "A crude incidence rate built from a registry numerator and a census denominator, compared across three levels."
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "application",
     "title": "Orient Yourself to Your Own Registry World",
     "content": "Before you code a single case, find out exactly where you sit in the pyramid. Work these six steps:\n\n**1. Find your state's cancer reporting law.** Search your state health department for \"cancer reporting rules\" — note who must report, how fast, and the penalties.\n\n**2. Pull your state's reportable list.** Every central registry publishes one. Note the **reference date** and any state-specific additions beyond the standard reportable neoplasms.\n\n**3. Determine your state's national affiliation.** Is your central registry SEER-funded, NPCR-funded, or both? Check the NCI SEER and CDC NPCR registry lists.\n\n**4. Check your facility's accreditation.** Is it **CoC-accredited**? If yes, its cases flow to NCDB and it must meet CoC abstracting and follow-up standards. It reports to the state either way.\n\n**5. Identify your registry software and NAACCR layout version.** Every abstract conforms to a versioned record layout — know which is in force this year.\n\n**6. Map your CTR eligibility path now.** Review the **NCRA** eligibility routes — formal education plus registry experience — and log hours from day one.\n\nWrite these six answers on one page and tape it above your monitor. Most \"where does this go?\" questions answer themselves once you can see the map.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">SIX STEPS TO FIND YOUR PLACE</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"23\">Answer these before you abstract your first case</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"150\" width=\"980\" height=\"120\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"210\" r=\"33\" fill=\"#6366f1\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"221\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"bold\">1</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"23\" font-weight=\"bold\">Find your state cancer reporting law</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"238\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"19\">Who reports, how fast, and what happens if they do not</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"285\" width=\"980\" height=\"120\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"345\" r=\"33\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"356\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"bold\">2</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"335\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"23\" font-weight=\"bold\">Pull the state reportable list</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"373\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"19\">Reference date plus any state-specific additions</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"420\" width=\"980\" height=\"120\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"480\" r=\"33\" fill=\"#3b82f6\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"491\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"bold\">3</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"470\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"23\" font-weight=\"bold\">Identify SEER, NPCR, or both</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"508\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"19\">This decides which national files contain your cases</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"555\" width=\"980\" height=\"120\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"615\" r=\"33\" fill=\"#f59e0b\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"626\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"bold\">4</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"605\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"23\" font-weight=\"bold\">Check CoC accreditation status</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"643\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"19\">Accredited means NCDB submission and CoC standards</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"690\" width=\"980\" height=\"120\" fill=\"rgba(20,184,166,0.15)\" stroke=\"#14b8a6\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"750\" r=\"33\" fill=\"#14b8a6\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"761\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"bold\">5</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"740\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"23\" font-weight=\"bold\">Learn your NAACCR layout version</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"778\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"19\">Every abstract conforms to a versioned record layout</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"825\" width=\"980\" height=\"120\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"885\" r=\"33\" fill=\"#10b981\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"896\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"bold\">6</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"875\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"23\" font-weight=\"bold\">Map your CTR eligibility path</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"913\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"19\">Log registry experience hours starting on day one</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"965\" width=\"980\" height=\"95\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"1022\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">Six answers on one page, taped above your monitor</text></svg>",
      "caption": "A six-step orientation checklist that locates you inside the state and national reporting structure."
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "quiz",
     "question": "A federal health researcher needs the 2022 incidence rate of pancreatic cancer for the entire United States population, broken out by state. Which data source is the appropriate choice?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "United States Cancer Statistics (USCS), which combines CDC's NPCR data with NCI's SEER data to cover essentially the whole U.S. population.",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "The National Cancer Database, since it collects cases from more than a thousand accredited hospitals and captures the large majority of newly diagnosed U.S. cancers.",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "SEER alone, because SEER is the gold-standard federal cancer dataset and its registries are the most rigorously quality-controlled.",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "The pooled annual reports of individual hospital cancer registries, compiled by NCRA from its member facilities.",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "An incidence rate requires a numerator of all cases occurring in a defined population and a matching census denominator. Only population-based registries provide that, and USCS is the combined NPCR plus SEER product built exactly for it. The NCDB answer is the trap: it holds a huge volume of cases and superb treatment detail, but it is hospital-based and limited to CoC-accredited programs, so it has no valid population denominator and cannot yield true incidence rates. SEER alone covers only part of the country and cannot report every state.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">WHY VOLUME IS NOT COVERAGE</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"23\">A rate needs a denominator, not just a lot of cases</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"150\" width=\"470\" height=\"430\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"285\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"26\" font-weight=\"bold\">POPULATION-BASED</text><text x=\"285\" y=\"243\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"19\">NPCR  •  SEER  •  USCS</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"280\" width=\"370\" height=\"110\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"12\"/><text x=\"285\" y=\"322\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Numerator: every case in</text><text x=\"285\" y=\"357\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">the defined geography</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"410\" width=\"370\" height=\"110\" fill=\"rgba(20,184,166,0.15)\" stroke=\"#14b8a6\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"12\"/><text x=\"285\" y=\"452\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Denominator: census</text><text x=\"285\" y=\"487\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">population of that area</text><text x=\"285\" y=\"552\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">RATES ARE VALID</text><rect x=\"580\" y=\"150\" width=\"470\" height=\"430\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"815\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"26\" font-weight=\"bold\">HOSPITAL-BASED</text><text x=\"815\" y=\"243\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"19\">NCDB</text><rect x=\"630\" y=\"280\" width=\"370\" height=\"110\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"12\"/><text x=\"815\" y=\"322\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Numerator: cases from</text><text x=\"815\" y=\"357\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">accredited programs</text><rect x=\"630\" y=\"410\" width=\"370\" height=\"110\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"12\"/><text x=\"815\" y=\"452\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Denominator: none.</text><text x=\"815\" y=\"487\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">No population to divide by</text><text x=\"815\" y=\"552\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">RATES ARE NOT VALID</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"625\" width=\"1000\" height=\"180\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"683\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"25\" font-weight=\"bold\">WHAT NCDB IS EXCELLENT FOR</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"727\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Treatment patterns, guideline concordance, survival by</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"765\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">stage, and comparing your hospital against its peers</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"850\" width=\"1000\" height=\"200\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"910\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"25\" font-weight=\"bold\">THE TEST QUESTION TO ASK YOURSELF</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"958\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"21\">Does this dataset know the size of the population</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"998\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"21\">its cases came out of? If not, no rate.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Population-based sources carry a valid denominator; hospital-based sources do not, no matter how many cases they hold."
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "concept",
     "title": "The Credential, and What You Build Next",
     "content": "The person who does this work is a **cancer registrar**, and the profession's credential is the **CTR — Certified Tumor Registrar**, granted through the **National Cancer Registrars Association (NCRA)** and its **Council on Certification**. Eligibility runs through defined routes combining formal education with documented registry experience; the exam tests exactly what this course teaches — casefinding, abstracting, coding, staging, treatment, follow-up, and statistics. CTRs maintain the credential through continuing education on a recurring two-year cycle.\n\nCoC-accredited programs are expected to have CTR-credentialed staff doing the abstracting, which is why the letters matter economically as well as professionally.\n\nHere is what the rest of this course builds:\n\n• **Reportability and casefinding** — deciding what is even a case\n• **ICD-O-3** topography and morphology coding, plus behavior and grade\n• **Multiple primary and histology rules** — the Solid Tumor and Hematopoietic rules that decide whether you have one cancer or two\n• **AJCC TNM, 8th edition** and **SEER Summary Stage 2018** — two staging systems, coded side by side\n• **First course of treatment** coding, surgery codes, and treatment sequencing\n• **Follow-up, death clearance, and quality control** — edits, visual editing, reabstracting studies\n• **Statistics and reporting** — age-adjusted rates, relative survival, stage distribution\n• **CoC standards** and the other specialized registries: trauma, transplant, birth defects, immunization\n\nEvery one of those skills exists to protect the same thing: the accuracy of a number a frightened person will read at 2 a.m. Let's go earn it.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">THE CREDENTIAL AND THE ROAD AHEAD</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"23\">What you are training for, and what comes next</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"140\" width=\"980\" height=\"260\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"bold\">CTR  —  CERTIFIED TUMOR REGISTRAR</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"252\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"21\">Credential of the National Cancer Registrars Association</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"292\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"21\">Awarded by the NCRA Council on Certification exam</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"332\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"21\">Eligibility: formal education plus registry experience</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"372\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"21\">Maintained by continuing education every two years</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"440\" width=\"300\" height=\"190\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"210\" y=\"492\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">REPORTABILITY</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"535\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Casefinding sources</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"570\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Reportable lists</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"605\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Ambiguous terminology</text><rect x=\"400\" y=\"440\" width=\"300\" height=\"190\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"492\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">ICD-O-3</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"535\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Topography codes</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"570\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Morphology codes</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"605\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Behavior and grade</text><rect x=\"740\" y=\"440\" width=\"300\" height=\"190\" fill=\"rgba(236,72,153,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ec4899\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"890\" y=\"492\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ec4899\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">MP / H RULES</text><text x=\"890\" y=\"535\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Solid Tumor rules</text><text x=\"890\" y=\"570\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Hematopoietic rules</text><text x=\"890\" y=\"605\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">One cancer or two?</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"655\" width=\"300\" height=\"190\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"210\" y=\"707\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">STAGING</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"750\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">AJCC TNM 8th edition</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"785\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Summary Stage 2018</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"820\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">EOD data items</text><rect x=\"400\" y=\"655\" width=\"300\" height=\"190\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"707\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">TREATMENT</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"750\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">First course coding</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"785\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Surgery code schemes</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"820\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Systemic therapy</text><rect x=\"740\" y=\"655\" width=\"300\" height=\"190\" fill=\"rgba(20,184,166,0.15)\" stroke=\"#14b8a6\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"890\" y=\"707\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14b8a6\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">DATA QUALITY</text><text x=\"890\" y=\"750\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Follow-up programs</text><text x=\"890\" y=\"785\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Edits and visual review</text><text x=\"890\" y=\"820\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Statistics and reporting</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"890\" width=\"980\" height=\"165\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"20\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"947\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"23\" font-weight=\"bold\">Plus the other specialized registries</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"992\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Trauma  •  Transplant  •  Birth defects  •  Immunization</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"1032\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"19\">Same discipline, different disease surveillance mission</text></svg>",
      "caption": "The CTR credential at the center, surrounded by the six skill domains this course develops."
     }
    }
