     "type": "quiz",
     "question": "A death certificate reads — Part I: (a) septic shock, 2 days; (b) obstructing sigmoid mass with perforation, 1 week; (c) adenocarcinoma of the sigmoid colon, 9 months. Part II: atrial fibrillation, chronic kidney disease stage 3. Which condition will be tabulated as the underlying cause of death in national mortality statistics?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "Septic shock, because it is the condition that directly caused death",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Adenocarcinoma of the sigmoid colon, because it is the disease that initiated the chain of events leading to death",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "Obstructing sigmoid mass with perforation, because it is the most clinically severe event in the sequence",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Chronic kidney disease stage 3, because Part II conditions are weighted into the final cause selection",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "The underlying cause of death is defined as the disease or injury that initiated the chain of morbid events leading directly to death — the bottom-most line of the Part I cascade. That is the colon adenocarcinoma. Septic shock is the tempting answer because it is what actually stopped the heart, but that is the **immediate cause**, and immediate causes (sepsis, respiratory failure, cardiac arrest) are deliberately not tabulated. Part II conditions are coded and retained but never selected as the underlying cause.",
